<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">WTI crude rose sharply from the February 28 conflict onset, reached a closing peak of $109.47 on May 5, then retreated as ceasefire optimism gave way to a grinding oscillation between escalation and de-escalation; the June 9-10 US strikes on Iran reversed a brief recovery and returned oil to the $88-89 range where it trades today.</p>

<h2>What the Research Shows About Selling into Volatility</h2>

<p>Brad Barber and Terrance Odean''s 2000 study in the Journal of Finance, tracking 10,000 individual brokerage accounts over seven years, found that the stocks investors sold outperformed the stocks they retained by an average of 3.4 percentage points over the following year. The disposition effect is not merely a theoretical construct. It has a measurable cost.</p>

<p>In the specific context of commodity-driven volatility, the research is even more pointed. A client who sells an energy position because WTI has dropped from $109 to $89 is making a decision about the last six weeks, not about the next six months. The war that drove the original rally is not resolved. The Strait of Hormuz remains substantially closed. Global crude inventory draws have been running at a pace not seen since the 1980s, with US stockpiles including strategic reserves falling more than 70 million barrels over five weeks through early June, according to EIA data. The physical oil market is tight. The war premium oscillates, but it has not disappeared.</p>

<p>The advisor who can name the disposition effect, explain its mechanism, and connect it to the client''s specific situation is providing something no news headline can replicate. They are giving the client a framework for understanding why the urge to sell feels rational and is not.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with concentrated energy positions are experiencing what behavioural economists call reference point updating. They booked the gain mentally at the April peak. Everything since feels like loss, even if the position is still substantially profitable. They are not panicking in the classic sense. They feel like they are being prudent by taking action before things get worse. That sense of rational self-protection is the most dangerous version of the disposition effect because it is the hardest to interrupt.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to walk through what is happening with your energy position, because I think the instinct you are feeling deserves a real response rather than just reassurance. The position is still up significantly from where we built it. What has changed is that oil pulled back from its April peak, and your brain has re-anchored to that peak rather than to your cost basis. Behavioural researchers call this the disposition effect. It means we systematically want to sell winners when they pull back, and the feeling of wanting to lock in the gain is indistinguishable from the feeling of wanting to avoid a loss. The facts underneath have not changed. The Strait of Hormuz is still largely closed. The war that created the original thesis is still active. The company fundamentals have improved. What I would like to do is hold the position and review it properly at the end of the month with updated data. If the thesis has genuinely changed, we will act. Right now, what the research tells me is that this urge to sell is the disposition effect running, not a signal from the market.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who added significant energy weight during the February-April rally and are sitting on large unrealized gains in non-registered accounts. The tax consequence of realizing now is material and avoidable if the thesis holds.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with energy through diversified balanced or income funds who are less aware of the sector allocation. They may not be calling, but the disposition effect is running in the background. They will call when the next headline hits.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with no energy exposure who are watching sector performance and wondering if they missed the move. A pullback from $109 to $89 can look like an entry point. This is the mirror of the disposition effect: recency bias suggesting the peak was normal and the current price is a discount.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review energy weighting across all client accounts and identify who holds more than 10% in the sector, either directly or through concentrated fund exposure.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients with significant non-registered gains, model the tax consequence of realizing at current prices versus holding through Q3. Present the numbers, not just the recommendation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document the original investment thesis for each major energy position. If the thesis was the Hormuz disruption and elevated WTI, confirm that thesis is still intact before any sell decision.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Schedule proactive calls with your top 10 clients in this category before they call you. The disposition effect accelerates when clients are left to self-manage through headline volatility.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a one-paragraph written rationale for holding. Clients who receive it in writing are less likely to override the recommendation on an emotional basis in the next 48 hours.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your energy position and what I want you to know about it<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to follow up on our conversation today with a brief note you can refer back to over the next few weeks.<br><br>
Your energy exposure is still significantly higher than where we built it. The instinct to reduce it after the pullback from April''s peak is completely understandable, and I want to acknowledge that rather than dismiss it. What I explained on the call is that this instinct has a name in the research: the disposition effect. It describes the tendency to sell profitable positions too early when they pull back, because our brains treat a retreat from a peak as a loss rather than as a position that is still well ahead.<br><br>
The underlying thesis when we added this exposure was the Hormuz disruption and elevated oil prices. As of today, the Strait remains largely closed. US military strikes on Iran resumed this week. The physical oil market continues to tighten based on inventory data. The thesis is intact.<br><br>
I will review this position with you formally at the end of the month with updated data. In the meantime, I am watching it closely. If anything changes materially, I will reach out immediately.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors with energy exposure:</strong> Self-directed investors who added oil sands names during the February-April rally are now watching WTI retreat with no framework for the decision in front of them. They have no one to call. The disposition effect is running at full strength, and they are likely to make the textbook error without a professional counterweight.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners and incorporated clients with appreciated securities:</strong> The capital gains inclusion rate remains at 50% in 2026 after the Carney cancellation. Clients who built a corporate investment account with energy exposure have a planning conversation available right now about timing of dispositions and the tax efficiency of the current structure.</p>
<p><strong>Recently retired clients managing their own transition:</strong> Clients who left their employers in the last two years and are now self-directing a portfolio for the first time are acutely susceptible to disposition effect triggers. Market volatility in a high-profile sector is exactly when they discover they needed an advisor.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am reaching out because I have been watching what is happening with Canadian energy stocks and I wanted to share a specific piece of research that is relevant to anyone holding a position right now. Would you have ten minutes?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The disposition effect is one of the most expensive mistakes in investing, and it is operating right now in the energy sector. WTI is down 20% from its April peak, but the underlying war thesis has not resolved. Clients without an advisor are likely to sell into this pullback after having waited through the rally, which is the worst possible sequence. The advisor''s value in this moment is not access to information. It is a framework for making the decision correctly, and the research-backed ability to interrupt an emotional response before it becomes a transaction.</p>
<p>The DIY investor who calls their online broker to sell their Suncor position today has no one to tell them they are running the disposition effect. That is a gap worth bridging.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How long have you held your energy names, and what was your original thesis for adding them?</p>
<p>When oil pulled back in May and again this week, what was your instinct, and did you act on it?</p>
<p>If WTI were to recover to $100 in the next 90 days, how would you feel about a decision to sell today at $89?</p>
<p>Do you have a written plan for how you manage positions when they pull back from a peak, or do you make those calls in the moment?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A research note on energy stocks and a specific mistake to avoid right now<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
I wanted to share a brief note that I think is directly relevant to anyone holding Canadian energy stocks after the pullback from April''s peak.<br><br>
There is a well-documented behavioural pattern called the disposition effect, identified in the research of Shefrin and Statman and grounded in Kahneman and Tversky''s prospect theory. It describes the systematic tendency of investors to sell profitable positions when they pull back from a high, treating the retreat from the peak as a loss rather than evaluating the position against its original cost basis and thesis.<br><br>
WTI crude has dropped from about $109 in late April to around $89 today. Investors who added energy exposure during the rally are now facing the exact psychological conditions the research identifies as a high-risk window for this error.<br><br>
If you are managing your energy positions on your own right now, I would be glad to spend 20 minutes walking through how to evaluate the decision correctly. No obligation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

