<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Bank of Canada, policy rate announcements 2024 to 2026. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The Bank of Canada cut 225 basis points between June 2024 and June 2025, then held at 2.25% through five consecutive decisions; yesterday''s hold was accompanied by explicit two-way language that puts both further cuts and consecutive hikes on the table depending on how the Middle East conflict and US tariff situation evolve.</p>

<h2>The Mortgage Renewal Decision Has Changed Since Yesterday Morning</h2>

<p>Approximately 1.15 million Canadian mortgages are renewing in 2026, according to Morningstar DBRS. Of those, five-year fixed-rate holders face estimated payment increases of 15% to 20%, because they locked in rates near or below 2% in 2021 and are now renewing in a 4.4% to 4.7% fixed-rate environment. Variable-rate holders face a different calculation: the Bank of Canada''s prime rate sits at 4.45%, variable mortgages are typically priced at prime minus a spread, and with the BoC explicitly on hold, the short-term certainty of the variable rate has improved.</p>

<p>The word "consecutive" in Macklem''s June 10 press conference deserves careful reading. He did not say the Bank would hike. He said that if energy prices start feeding into broader inflation, monetary policy will have more work to do and "there may be a need for consecutive increases." CIBC senior economist Andrew Grantham noted after the press conference that the "consecutive" language plays into market perceptions that the Bank is more concerned about upside inflation risks than downside growth risks. Markets are currently pricing in a quarter-point hike before year-end.</p>

<p>For a client renewing a $600,000 mortgage in September, the fixed-versus-variable decision now carries a potential downside scenario it did not carry 48 hours ago. A quarter-point hike would add approximately $90 per month to a variable-rate mortgage at that balance. Two quarter-point hikes would add $180. The planning conversation is not about predicting what the Bank will do. It is about what risk the client is willing to carry, and whether the TFSA buffer they hold is large enough to absorb a payment shock if the hike scenario materializes.</p>

<h2>The Capital Gains Cancellation and What It Still Means for Tax Planning in 2026</h2>

<p>The 50% inclusion rate is now confirmed permanent for the foreseeable future following PM Carney''s March 2025 cancellation of the proposed 66.67% increase. The Lifetime Capital Gains Exemption has been raised to $1.25 million for qualifying small business shares and farming and fishing property, effective June 25, 2024. Those two data points together define the capital gains planning landscape for 2026.</p>

<p>For clients with appreciated non-registered portfolios, the tax efficiency of holding growth-oriented investments outside registered accounts has improved relative to what was feared in 2024. But it has not changed the fundamental hierarchy: RRSP and TFSA room should be deployed before non-registered capital is used for long-term savings. What has changed is the order of priority within the registered bucket. In the current environment, the TFSA''s advantage on fixed income is not theoretical. It is measurable, and for clients in the top marginal bracket, it is worth a direct planning conversation about asset location rather than only about contribution room.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients renewing mortgages are anxious. They received Macklem''s "two-way risk" language as more uncertainty in an environment that already feels uncertain. Clients who were leaning toward variable rates because "the Bank is on hold" now feel less certain. Clients who locked fixed in anticipation of hikes feel briefly vindicated. Neither group is approaching the decision with full information. The planning gap is real and the window to close it is the weeks immediately before their renewal date.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Bank of Canada held yesterday as expected, but the press conference added something worth discussing. Macklem used the phrase "consecutive increases" for the first time in this cycle when describing what might happen if energy-driven inflation starts spreading. That does not mean hikes are coming. The base case is still a hold through 2026. But it does mean that if you are renewing a mortgage in the next six months, the risk profile on a variable rate is not as clean as it was a week ago. Here is what I want to walk through with you. First, what is your actual payment tolerance if the variable rate moves 50 basis points higher by year-end? Second, is your TFSA contribution room fully deployed, and if not, is the right asset to hold in there right now a GIC or a money market fund rather than equities, as a buffer against that scenario? These are the two questions the June 10 decision opened up that were not as pressing before yesterday.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients renewing five-year fixed mortgages set in 2021 at sub-2% rates. They are facing the largest payment increases regardless of the rate path, and the TFSA-as-buffer conversation is most urgent for this group.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients already on variable rates at prime minus a spread. Yesterday''s hold is neutral for their current payment, but the "consecutive hikes" language introduces a tail risk they should be stress-testing against their cash flow.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who hold significant fixed income in non-registered accounts and have unused TFSA room. The asset location conversation, moving taxable interest income into the TFSA, is most valuable for top-bracket clients with 3.5% to 4.0% GIC positions sitting outside registered accounts.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull a list of all clients with mortgage renewals in the next 120 days. For each, confirm whether they hold a fixed or variable rate and at what original rate. The size of the payment shock at renewal varies enormously depending on what they locked in 2021.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For each renewing client, run the variable-rate scenario at current prime (4.45%) plus a 0.50% shock. Present both the current variable payment and the shocked payment before they make the fixed-versus-variable decision.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review TFSA contribution room for your top 20 clients. Identify who has unused room and what they are currently holding in non-registered accounts that could be migrated to reduce taxable income.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For incorporated clients, confirm whether the $1.25 million LCGE applies to any planned share dispositions in 2026. The increased threshold is now confirmed and can be incorporated into succession planning timelines.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Send a brief note to renewing mortgage clients today acknowledging the June 10 decision and confirming a call to review the fixed-versus-variable decision with the updated language from Macklem''s press conference.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Bank of Canada''s June 10 language and what it means for your mortgage renewal<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
As you know, the Bank of Canada held its overnight rate at 2.25% yesterday for the fifth consecutive time. I wanted to flag something specific from Governor Macklem''s press conference that is relevant to your mortgage renewal in the coming months.<br><br>
Macklem introduced what he described as two-way risk: the Bank may cut if the economy weakens under tariff pressure, or it may raise rates consecutively if energy-driven inflation starts spreading into broader prices. Markets are currently pricing in a small probability of a quarter-point hike before year-end.<br><br>
This does not change my overall assessment that the base case remains a hold through 2026. But it does mean the fixed-versus-variable decision at your renewal carries a slightly different risk profile than it did a week ago. I would like to walk through the numbers with you, including what a 0.25% to 0.50% rate increase would mean for your monthly payment under a variable structure, before you finalize your choice.<br><br>
I will reach out to schedule that call. In the meantime, please do not sign a renewal offer from your lender without speaking with me first.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Self-directed mortgage holders approaching renewal:</strong> Clients managing their own renewal without broker advice are the most likely to accept the lender''s posted renewal rate, which is almost never their best option. The June 10 decision and its "consecutive hikes" language creates a legitimate reason to reach out.</p>
<p><strong>High-income professionals with unused TFSA room:</strong> Physicians, lawyers, senior executives, and business owners who have prioritized RRSP over TFSA and are holding fixed income in non-registered accounts are leaving meaningful after-tax return on the table. The asset location conversation is the entry point.</p>
<p><strong>Small business owners with corporate investment accounts:</strong> The LCGE increase to $1.25 million for qualifying small business shares is now confirmed. Business owners approaching succession or considering a sale of shares have a planning window that many have not yet engaged with their current advisor.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The Bank of Canada held rates yesterday as expected, but the Governor''s press conference added a new element that directly affects the mortgage renewal decision you are going to be making in the next few months. I wanted to make sure you had the full picture before you received your lender''s renewal offer.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A mortgage renewal is one of the few financial decisions Canadians make where the lender''s default offer is almost never optimal, and where the difference between accepting it and negotiating or switching can be $5,000 to $15,000 over the term. The Bank of Canada''s June 10 language has made the fixed-versus-variable choice more nuanced than it was 48 hours ago. An advisor who can run the stress-test scenario and frame the decision correctly is providing value that is immediately measurable in dollars.</p>
<p>The TFSA conversation is complementary. For a client in the top marginal bracket, moving $50,000 of GIC interest income from a non-registered account into a TFSA saves approximately $900 per year in tax at a 3.75% yield. That is a compounding advantage that accumulates every year the asset remains in the correct location.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>When is your mortgage renewing, and have you already received a renewal offer from your lender?</p>
<p>When you locked in your current rate, was it a fixed or variable product, and what rate did you secure?</p>
<p>Have you reviewed your TFSA contribution room recently, and do you know what you are currently holding inside the account?</p>
<p>Are you aware that the stress test requirement has been removed for renewals where you stay with your current lender, which means you have more negotiating flexibility than you may realize?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Bank of Canada''s June 10 decision and your mortgage renewal<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada held its overnight rate at 2.25% yesterday, but Governor Macklem''s press conference introduced language that directly affects the fixed-versus-variable decision for anyone renewing a mortgage in 2026.<br><br>
Specifically, Macklem said the Bank may need "consecutive increases" if energy-driven inflation starts spreading more broadly. Markets are now pricing in a small probability of a hike before year-end. For anyone renewing in the next 90 to 120 days, this changes the risk profile of the variable-rate option in a way that is worth stress-testing before signing.<br><br>
If your mortgage is coming up for renewal and you are making that decision without a second set of eyes on the numbers, I would be glad to spend 20 minutes running through the fixed-versus-variable scenario with updated data from yesterday''s decision. No obligation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

