He described the Bank as caught between two competing risks: raising rates to fight inflation could deepen the economic slowdown from tariff uncertainty, while cutting rates to support growth could let energy-driven inflation become entrenched. He used the word "consecutive" when describing potential hikes if core inflation starts moving up. That is a significant shift in tone from what we have heard over the previous four holds.<br><br>
The next decision point is June 22, when Statistics Canada releases May CPI data. That number will tell us whether the Bank''s "look-through" logic on energy inflation remains valid or whether the hike scenario is becoming the base case.<br><br>
If you are managing any rate-sensitive financial decisions without a professional second opinion right now, I would be glad to spend 20 minutes reviewing the June 10 decision and what it means for your specific situation.<br><br>
