<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">WTI crude surged from the pre-conflict range near $75 to a peak of $109.47 in early May, then retraced as ceasefire optimism and partial Hormuz flows reduced the acute risk premium; the June 9-10 US strikes reversed the retracement and returned oil to the $88-89 range, with the market now pricing a persistent moderate disruption rather than either resolution or full escalation.</p>

<h2>The Canadian Portfolio Implication Is More Nuanced Than the Headline</h2>

<p>For Canadian investors, the Hormuz conflict has had two simultaneous and partially offsetting effects. The TSX energy sub-index has significantly outperformed the broader composite since late February, with Suncor posting Q1 net income of C$2.1 billion against C$1.7 billion a year earlier and CNQ also exceeding consensus estimates. The Trans Mountain Pipeline expansion, completed in 2024, has provided Canadian crude producers with improved access to Pacific markets, reducing their historical discount to WTI and insulating them somewhat from the Hormuz-specific disruption. Canadian oil sands production is not passing through the Strait. The conflict supports Canadian energy prices through the global WTI benchmark, not through direct supply risk.</p>

<p>The offsetting effect operates through the broader economy. Higher energy prices have pushed Canadian CPI to 2.8% in April, constrained the Bank of Canada''s ability to cut rates in support of a tariff-weakened economy, and added real costs to Canadian households and businesses that are not energy producers. The net effect on the TSX composite has been muted: energy has outperformed while rate-sensitive sectors, financials and utilities, and trade-exposed sectors have underperformed. The composite is roughly flat over the conflict period after accounting for both dynamics.</p>

<h2>What a Second Ceasefire Would Look Like and Why It Is Harder Than the First</h2>

<p>The conditions that made the April 8 ceasefire possible, a two-week pause mediated by Pakistan following a credible US military campaign, have not been recreated. Iran''s new leadership following the killing of Ali Khamenei is less centralized than the prior regime, making unified decision-making on concessions more difficult. The IAEA''s board of governors has passed a resolution demanding Iran declare its remaining enriched uranium stockpile and permit inspections, which Tehran has rejected. The US counter-blockade on Iranian port shipping remains in place alongside the Iranian closure of Hormuz to international tankers.</p>

<p>A second ceasefire is possible. Pakistan and Oman have both maintained back-channel communications. The UK, Germany, and France have called publicly for a long-term agreement. But the structural obstacles are higher than they were in early April, and the June 9-10 exchange has added fresh grievances to the negotiating table. The base case for the next 30 to 60 days is continued disruption at current intensity, meaning WTI in the $85 to $95 range, with tail risk in both directions depending on whether strikes target energy infrastructure or a diplomatic opening emerges.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with energy exposure are watching the news cycle and trying to determine whether the June 9-10 exchange is the beginning of something worse or another oscillation in a pattern they have already experienced since February. Clients without energy exposure are watching the broader TSX underperform global benchmarks and asking whether Canada is more exposed to the conflict than other markets. Both groups are operating with incomplete frameworks for the question in front of them.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">What happened this week is significant but not a rupture from the pattern we have been tracking since February. The ceasefire that held briefly in April has now collapsed, and US and Iranian forces exchanged strikes for the second day running on Wednesday. The market priced a conflict premium when this started, then partially unwound it on ceasefire optimism, and is now repricing it again. The current WTI price of around $89 reflects a middle scenario: continued disruption, no full resolution, no major escalation targeting infrastructure. The two risks that are not priced are a strike on Gulf energy infrastructure, which would spike oil well above the April peak, and a durable new ceasefire, which J.P. Morgan estimates would bring WTI back toward $60. Both are tail risks. The base case is more of what we have seen over the last three months. For your portfolio, that base case is already reflected in our current energy weighting, and I am comfortable with that positioning through at least the July 15 Bank of Canada meeting.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated TSX energy exposure who are now watching a second wave of conflict news after having held through the ceasefire retracement. The disposition effect is running alongside the geopolitical uncertainty, creating a double pressure to reduce positions.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with globally diversified equity portfolios. The conflict has mixed effects across global equities: European energy companies benefit, Asian manufacturers face higher input costs, and US equities face both an inflation headwind and an energy sector tailwind.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with gold exposure. Gold has continued to trade at elevated levels through the conflict, reflecting both geopolitical risk premium and the safe-haven demand that accompanies sustained uncertainty. Agnico Eagle and Wheaton Precious Metals are two Canadian names that have benefited.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
