<div class="checklist-item">Review the geopolitical risk component of each client''s portfolio positioning against the current conflict timeline. The April ceasefire unraveled on a specific sequence of events that is now documented. Use that sequence to stress-test the "base case holds" assumption in your positioning.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients with energy exposure, prepare a written summary of the two tail-risk scenarios (infrastructure strike versus durable ceasefire) and their estimated portfolio impact. Clients who receive this framing are less likely to react emotionally to the next headline.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Monitor the May CPI release on June 22 in the context of the June 9-10 escalation. If WTI remains in the $88 to $95 range through June, May CPI will likely confirm the energy-driven inflation narrative. If it begins to show core price acceleration, the BoC rate path and bond market positioning both require reassessment.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Watch for any diplomatic signals from Pakistan, Oman, or the European troika. The path to a second ceasefire runs through those channels, and early signals will move markets before the formal announcement. Preposition clients to hold rather than react.</div>
</div>
