  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors tracking the conflict in the news:</strong> Self-directed investors who have been following the Hormuz conflict through financial media are receiving a disorganized set of signals: ceasefire optimism, then escalation, then partial recovery, now re-escalation. Without a framework for the probability distribution across outcomes, they are making portfolio decisions reactively rather than analytically. The advisor''s value here is the framework itself.</p>
<p><strong>Investors with global equity exposure through balanced funds:</strong> Clients in balanced or global equity funds who are not aware of their energy sector exposure are carrying the conflict risk without understanding it. The fund manager is making the positioning decision, but the client bears the consequence. An advisor who can explain the exposure is providing a service the fund statement does not.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with fuel-cost exposure:</strong> Owners of transportation, logistics, or manufacturing businesses for whom elevated oil prices represent a direct operating cost increase have both a personal investment planning conversation and a business planning conversation available. Higher fuel costs are reducing margins on the business side while the same conflict is supporting returns on the energy side of a portfolio.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I have been tracking the Hormuz conflict since it started in February and I wanted to reach out after this week''s exchange of strikes, because I think the situation has moved in a way that is worth understanding clearly before making any portfolio decisions.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The Hormuz conflict has produced a market environment where the correct portfolio response depends entirely on which scenario is assigned the higher probability: infrastructure escalation, continued moderate disruption, or diplomatic resolution. Those three scenarios have dramatically different implications for energy equities, bond yields, and the Canadian dollar. An investor without a framework for assigning those probabilities is making portfolio decisions at random relative to the outcome that actually matters. The advisor who has tracked the conflict from the beginning, who understands the ceasefire structure and why it collapsed, and who can articulate the tail risks in dollar terms is providing an analytical service that no financial news outlet replicates.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
