VALUES (
  '2026/06/11/tsx-splits-on-the-boc-hold-energy-holds-everything-else-folds',
  'market', 'article',
  'TSX Splits on the BoC Hold: Energy Holds, Everything Else Folds',
  'The TSX composite fell approximately 0.3% on Wednesday as base metals, technology, and rate-sensitive sectors absorbed the Bank of Canada''s two-way risk language while energy names partially offset the decline on renewed Hormuz escalation. The session illustrated a structural divide in the Canadian market that has persisted since late February.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The S&P/TSX Composite Index fell approximately 106 points to close near 34,305 on June 10,</strong><span> a decline of roughly 0.3%, as base metals and technology led losses while the energy sector held firm on renewed US-Iran hostilities pushing WTI toward $89.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Bank of Canada''s fifth consecutive hold at 2.25% did not provide the relief rally some had anticipated.</strong><span> Governor Macklem''s "consecutive hikes" language at the press conference weighed on rate-sensitive financials and utilities, offsetting any relief from the hold itself.</span></li>
<li><strong>WTI crude traded near $88 to $89 on the session,</strong><span> supported by the June 9-10 US strikes on Iran and Iranian retaliatory action, but unable to recover the $90 level as markets digested news that the latest strikes had not targeted energy infrastructure.</span></li>
<li><strong>US markets fell sharply in sympathy,</strong><span> with the Dow Jones Industrial Average dropping approximately 509 points and the S&P 500 losing roughly 52 points, as US inflation concerns and renewed Hormuz escalation weighed on risk sentiment globally.</span></li>
<li><strong>Suncor''s Q1 2026 net income reached C$2.1 billion,</strong><span> up from C$1.7 billion a year earlier, with adjusted funds from operations rising 32%. The stock has returned approximately 98% over the past 12 months against CNQ''s 59%, reflecting the integrated model''s advantage at elevated crude prices.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The TSX composite closed Wednesday near 34,305, down approximately 106 points or 0.3%, in a session that illustrated the structural divide defining Canadian equities since the Hormuz conflict began. Energy names held as WTI crude traded near $88 to $89 per barrel following the second consecutive day of US military strikes on Iran. Base metals, technology, and rate-sensitive financials declined as the Bank of Canada''s press conference introduced "consecutive hikes" language that the market had not expected in such direct terms.</p>

<p>The split is not new. Since late February, the TSX has been effectively two markets trading in the same index. The energy sub-index has significantly outperformed the composite, supported by oil prices running 20% above pre-conflict levels. Everything else, financials constrained by rate uncertainty, materials exposed to global growth risk from tariff disruption, technology facing valuation pressure from elevated bond yields, has underperformed or moved sideways. Wednesday''s session compressed that dynamic into a single day.</p>

<h2>The BoC Press Conference and Its Market Effect</h2>

<p>The Bank of Canada''s hold at 2.25% was fully priced before the announcement. What was not fully priced was the specific language Governor Macklem used at the 10:30 AM press conference. His statement that monetary policy may need "consecutive increases" if energy-driven inflation spreads into broader prices landed on rate-sensitive sectors with immediate force.</p>

<p>Canadian bank stocks, which had been stable through the morning, slipped as bond yields moved modestly higher in response to the hike language. Utilities, which carry significant rate sensitivity through their capital structures, underperformed. The five-year Government of Canada bond yield, which drives fixed mortgage rates and represents the discount rate for rate-sensitive equities, edged up after the press conference as markets priced a slightly higher probability of a hike before year-end. CIBC economist Andrew Grantham noted that the "consecutive" language plays into market perceptions that the Bank is more worried about upside inflation risks than downside growth risks, a framing that is modestly negative for rate-sensitive equity sectors.</p>

<p>The irony is that the hold itself, the fifth consecutive decision to leave rates unchanged, should have been marginally supportive for rate-sensitive names. The language surrounding the hold negated that support. Macklem''s candour about the policy dilemma, which the Economy Desk covered separately today, translated directly into equity market uncertainty about the direction of the next move.</p>

<div class="hdq-chart">
