<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The TSX composite reached an all-time high of 34,544 on April 27, driven by energy sector re-rating on elevated WTI crude, then consolidated as ceasefire optimism partially unwound the oil premium; the June 10 session closed at 34,305, down 106 points, as BoC two-way risk language weighed on rate-sensitive sectors and the re-escalation of US-Iran hostilities kept energy names supported but not advancing.</p>

<h2>Energy Sector Divergence: Suncor, CNQ, and the Integrated Advantage</h2>

<p>Within the energy sector, Wednesday''s session highlighted a performance divergence between integrated producers and pure-play upstream names. Suncor, which posted Q1 net income of C$2.1 billion against C$1.7 billion a year earlier, has returned approximately 98% over the past 12 months. CNQ has returned approximately 59% over the same period, also strong but demonstrably lower than Suncor''s integrated-model performance. The divergence reflects Suncor''s downstream refining and retail operations, which benefit from elevated crack spreads as refined product prices track oil prices while feed costs are partially hedged through its own production.</p>

<p>Canadian oil sands producers carry a structural advantage in the current conflict that is worth stating clearly for client conversations: their production does not pass through the Strait of Hormuz. The conflict supports their revenue through the global WTI benchmark price while their physical supply chain is insulated from the direct disruption. The Trans Mountain Pipeline expansion, completed in 2024, has further diversified their export channels toward Pacific Basin buyers. That structural position is distinct from Middle Eastern producers and from global integrated majors with significant Gulf exposure.</p>

<h2>US Markets and the Canadian Read-Through</h2>

<p>US equity markets fell sharply on Wednesday. The Dow Jones Industrial Average dropped approximately 509 points to close near 50,363. The S&P 500 lost roughly 52 points to close near 7,335. The Nasdaq fell approximately 275 points. The US declines reflected two simultaneous pressures: inflation data and the Hormuz re-escalation. US household inflation has been pushed above 4% by higher energy prices, according to AP reporting, creating a Federal Reserve dilemma that mirrors the Bank of Canada''s own policy bind.</p>

<p>For Canadian portfolios, the US market weakness carries a specific read-through. Canadian equities have a structural correlation to US markets through overlapping capital flows, currency dynamics, and export dependency. When the S&P 500 falls 0.7%, the TSX typically moves in the same direction. Wednesday''s relative TSX outperformance, down only 0.3% against the S&P 500''s 0.7% decline, reflects the energy sector buffer that Canadian investors hold through the TSX''s 16% energy weight. That buffer is real, but it is not permanent. If WTI retreats toward $75 on a ceasefire, the TSX loses its relative insulation and reverts to normal correlation with US markets at a time when US markets face their own growth and inflation headwinds.</p>

<h2>CAD/USD and the Currency Overlay</h2>

<p>The Canadian dollar has traded in a range that reflects the competing forces of elevated oil prices, which support CAD, and tariff-driven economic weakness, which pressures it. Higher WTI is structurally positive for the Canadian dollar through the terms-of-trade channel. The Bank of Canada''s hold, removing near-term rate cut expectations, provides a modest additional support. Against those tailwinds, the tariff uncertainty and the Bank''s characterization of the Canadian economy as "weak" weigh on the currency. The net result has been a CAD that has not fully captured the oil price upside, a dynamic that professional currency managers have noted as a potential mean-reversion opportunity if the conflict sustains current oil levels through the summer.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients watching the TSX are receiving a mixed signal: the index is near all-time highs set in April, which should feel positive, but the most recent sessions have been choppy and the news backdrop is heavy. Clients with energy exposure feel differently from clients without it, and that divergence within a single portfolio, where the energy sleeve is up substantially and everything else is flat or down, creates a rebalancing conversation that advisors need to be prepared for.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Here is what Wednesday''s session actually showed. The TSX fell about 0.3%, which looks mild, but that number hides a sharp split underneath it. Energy held or gained because the Hormuz escalation is keeping oil near $89. Everything else, banks, tech, materials, pulled back because the Bank of Canada''s press conference introduced rate-hike language the market had not been pricing. The TSX composite is still within about 1.5% of its all-time high set in April. That is a reasonable place to be given everything that is happening. What I want to make sure is that your positioning across sectors reflects a deliberate view rather than an accidental outcome of the last few months. Your energy names have significantly outperformed. The question for the next quarter is whether that outperformance continues, stabilizes, or mean-reverts, and that depends on a conflict resolution timeline that nobody can predict precisely. What I can do is make sure we are not over-concentrated in any single outcome.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who are overweight energy relative to their target allocation. The TSX''s energy sub-index outperformance since February has likely drifted many balanced portfolios above their target energy weight without deliberate action. The rebalancing conversation is both a risk management and a tax planning event in non-registered accounts.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients in broad Canadian equity index funds or ETFs. They hold the 16% energy weight implicitly and have benefited from it, but they also hold the underperforming sectors that pulled the composite lower on Wednesday. Their experience mirrors the composite: slightly positive over the conflict period, but not as strong as a pure energy bet would have been.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who held gold as a portfolio diversifier. Agnico Eagle and Wheaton Precious Metals have both benefited from elevated geopolitical risk premiums. The gold safe-haven thesis has worked as intended in this environment.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Run a sector attribution analysis on each client''s Canadian equity exposure. Identify which clients have drifted above their target energy weight since February and flag those accounts for a rebalancing review before the July 15 BoC meeting.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients holding individual energy names with large unrealized gains in non-registered accounts, model the tax cost of rebalancing now versus the risk of a significant oil price decline on a new ceasefire. Present both scenarios with dollar estimates before recommending action.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review currency overlay for clients with significant USD exposure. The CAD has not fully captured the oil price upside. If the conflict sustains current oil levels, CAD strength is a potential headwind for unhedged USD positions.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag the next significant data events: June 22 (May CPI), July 15 (BoC decision and MPR). Both are inflection points for rate-sensitive equity sectors and should be in every client communication calendar.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Wednesday''s TSX session: what the split between energy and everything else means<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
The TSX composite fell about 0.3% on Wednesday, but that number understates the sector divergence underneath it. Energy names held up as renewed US-Iran hostilities kept oil near $89. Rate-sensitive sectors including banks and utilities slipped after the Bank of Canada''s press conference introduced language about potential consecutive rate hikes if energy inflation broadens.<br><br>
The composite is still within roughly 1.5% of its April all-time high of 34,544. That is a resilient position given the volume of uncertainty currently in the market.<br><br>
What I am watching over the next few weeks: the May CPI release on June 22, which will tell us whether the Bank''s rate-hike language has a data foundation, and the July 15 Bank of Canada decision, which will be accompanied by the first Monetary Policy Report since the June escalation. Both are potential inflection points for the rate-sensitive parts of the TSX.<br><br>
I will be in touch. In the meantime, please reach out if you have questions about any specific holdings.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors managing TSX exposure without sector awareness:</strong> Self-directed investors holding broad Canadian index ETFs may not realize they are carrying 16% energy weight, or that their recent performance has been driven almost entirely by the conflict premium. When that premium unwinds on a ceasefire, their returns will look very different. The advisor''s value is the sector literacy the index fund does not provide.</p>
<p><strong>Investors recently retired or approaching retirement with significant non-registered Canadian equity:</strong> Clients who need to draw income from non-registered accounts in the next one to three years are carrying equity risk at a moment when a significant resolution event, a ceasefire, could reprice the TSX energy sector by 20% or more. The conversation is about sequencing risk, not just market risk.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with concentrated Canadian equity through employee ownership or CCPC holdings:</strong> Business owners who have accumulated TSX exposure through corporate investment accounts or deferred compensation are potentially over-concentrated in Canadian equities at a moment when that concentration is heavily influenced by a single geopolitical event.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I wanted to reach out after Wednesday''s session because the TSX headline number, down 0.3%, does not tell the full story of what is happening in the Canadian market right now, and I think the underlying split is directly relevant to how your portfolio is positioned.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The TSX composite''s near-all-time-high level masks a sector divide that has been building since late February. Energy has carried the index; everything else has been flat to down. An investor who holds a broad Canadian equity index is implicitly making a bet on the Hormuz conflict persisting at current intensity, and most of them do not know it. When the conflict resolves, the index will reprice, and the clients who understood their exposure in advance will be positioned to manage the transition. Those who did not will be responding to a decline rather than anticipating it.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you know what your current allocation to the Canadian energy sector is, either through direct holdings or through index funds?</p>
<p>How has your Canadian equity portfolio performed relative to the TSX composite since February, and do you know the main driver of the difference?</p>
<p>If oil prices fell 20% on a ceasefire announcement, what would that mean for the Canadian equity portion of your portfolio in dollar terms?</p>
<p>Do you have a rebalancing discipline that triggers when a sector drifts significantly above its target weight, or do you manage that on a judgment basis?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The TSX is near an all-time high but the story underneath it is more complicated<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The S&P/TSX Composite closed Wednesday at 34,305, within about 1.5% of its April all-time high. If you are holding a broad Canadian equity index fund, that looks reassuring.<br><br>
What the headline number does not show is the sector split driving it. Energy names have significantly outperformed since the Hormuz conflict began in late February, carrying the index while banks, technology, and materials have been flat to down. The TSX''s current level is effectively a bet that the conflict continues at current intensity. A durable ceasefire that normalizes oil prices would be materially negative for the energy-heavy Canadian index, while a further escalation targeting Gulf infrastructure would be positive.<br><br>
If you are managing Canadian equity exposure without a clear view of how that conflict exposure is distributed across your holdings, I think it is worth a conversation. I would be glad to spend 20 minutes walking through what your current TSX position is actually exposed to.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
